{
 "id": 145,
 "created_at": "2026-10-27T01:08:58.218327+05:30",
 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dbi51cRsbSq/",
 "title": "How to Answer Tell Me About Yourself in Interviews",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that most people fail the interview's first question, 'Tell me about yourself,' by simply reading out their CV. A great answer should instead tell one specific story connecting your past experience to the current role and explain why you belong there.",
 "key_points": [
  "Do not answer 'Tell me about yourself' by stating your name, college, city, or years of experience, because that is just reading your CV out loud and the interviewer already has your CV.",
  "The creator claims that this question is a first test and most people fail it before the interview has even started.",
  "A great answer tells one specific story that explains who you are, what you are good at, and why you are sitting in that chair.",
  "Example answer: 'I've spent the last three years building sales teams from scratch in early startups. I've made every mistake possible, learned what actually moves the numbers, and I am now at a point where I want to bring that experience to a company that is ready to scale. That is why I applied here.'",
  "Keep it to 60 seconds with no hometown or college name unless it is relevant.",
  "Focus on clarity, confidence, and the reason you belong in the room.",
  "The creator claims the first 30 seconds of an interview always sets the tone for the whole thing."
 ],
 "content": "Never introduce yourself in an interview like this my friend. The interview starts with the most underrated question. Tell me about yourself. The most people answer it in the exact same way. My name is this. I studied at this college. I am from the city. I have this many years of experience. Thank you. That is not an introduction. That is CV being read out loud. The interviewer already has your CV. They do not need you to narrate it back to them. This question is not a formality. It is a first test and most people fail it before the interview has even started. Here is what a great answer to this question actually does. It tells a story, not your whole life story, but one specific story that explains who you are, what you're good at, and why you're sitting in this chair. It connects your past to this exact role in a way that makes the interviewer think, 'This person knows exactly why they are here.' Something like this. I've spent the last three years building sales teams from scratch in early startups. I've made every mistake possible. Learn what actually moves the numbers and I am now at a point where I want to bring that experience to a company that is ready to scale. That is why I applied here. 60 seconds. No hometown, no college name unless it is relevant. Just clarity, confidence and the reason you belong in this room. The first 30 seconds of an interview always sets the on for For the whole thing.",
 "status": "done",
